## Problem

The `approved-and-green` reaction with `action: auto-merge` does NOT actually merge PRs. The lifecycle manager's reaction handler at `packages/core/src/lifecycle-manager.ts` line ~725 has:

```typescript
case "auto-merge": {
  // Auto-merge is handled by the SCM plugin
  // For now, just notify
  const event = createEvent("reaction.triggered", { ... });
  await notifyHuman(event, "action", pollStats);
  return { ... action: "auto-merge", ... };
}
```

This means when a PR has CI green + no review blockers, ao fires a notification webhook instead of merging. The merge only happens if an external system (webhook consumer) picks up the event and runs `gh pr merge`. If that external system fails, the pipeline stalls silently.

## Impact

Pipeline #132 stalled for 24+ minutes because:
1. ao detected PR #220 was mergeable, fired `reaction.triggered`
2. The webhook consumer (OpenClaw hook session) received the event but failed silently
3. The cron backup also failed (timeout + delivery config issues)
4. No one merged the PR until a human noticed

## Fix

In the `auto-merge` case of `handleReaction()`, call the SCM plugin's merge function directly:

```typescript
case "auto-merge": {
  const project = config.projects[session.projectId];
  const scm = project?.scm ? registry.get<SCM>("scm", project.scm.plugin) : null;

  if (scm && session.pr) {
    try {
      await scm.merge(session.pr, { method: "squash", deleteBranch: true });
      // Update session status to merged
      updateSessionStatus(session, SESSION_STATUS.MERGED);
      const event = createEvent("merge.completed", {
        sessionId,
        projectId,
        message: `Auto-merged PR via approved-and-green reaction`,
        data: { reactionKey, pr: session.pr.url },
      });
      await notifyHuman(event, "action", pollStats);
    } catch (error) {
      // Fall back to notification if merge fails
      const event = createEvent("reaction.triggered", {
        sessionId,
        projectId,
        message: `Auto-merge failed for : ${error}. Manual merge needed.`,
        data: { reactionKey, error: String(error) },
      });
      await notifyHuman(event, "urgent", pollStats);
    }
  }

  return { reactionType: reactionKey, success: true, action: "auto-merge", escalated: false };
}
```

## Requirements

- [ ] Check if the SCM plugin interface already has a `merge()` method. If not, add one.
- [ ] The GitHub SCM plugin should implement merge using `gh pr merge --squash --delete-branch` (or the GitHub API equivalent)
- [ ] On merge success: transition session to `merged` status, fire `merge.completed` event
- [ ] On merge failure: fire notification at `urgent` priority so the webhook consumer can intervene
- [ ] Add a config option for merge method (`squash`, `merge`, `rebase`) defaulting to `squash`
- [ ] Add tests for the auto-merge path
- [ ] Run full test suite before pushing

## Notes
- The SCM plugin interface is at `packages/core/src/types.ts` or similar
- The GitHub SCM plugin is at `packages/plugins/scm-github/`
- Use conventional commits
- Include "Closes #THIS_ISSUE" in the commit

## Bug Description

`ao start <project>` always fails with the following error, even when dependencies are properly installed and built:

```
$ ao start passive

Starting orchestrator for passive

Error: Dependencies not installed. Run: pnpm install && pnpm build
```

Running `pnpm install && pnpm build` does not resolve the issue. The error persists for all configured projects.

## Root Cause

The preflight check in `packages/cli/src/lib/preflight.ts` (`checkBuilt`) uses a **hardcoded path** to verify `@composio/ao-core` is installed:

```ts
async function checkBuilt(webDir: string): Promise<void> {
  const nodeModules = resolve(webDir, "node_modules", "@composio", "ao-core");
  if (!existsSync(nodeModules)) {
    throw new Error("Dependencies not installed. Run: pnpm install && pnpm build");
  }
  // ...
}
```

This checks exactly one location: `<webDir>/node_modules/@composio/ao-core`.

- **pnpm monorepo (works):** pnpm symlinks workspace dependencies directly into each package's `node_modules`, so the path exists.
- **npm global install (broken):** npm **hoists** dependencies to a parent `node_modules` directory. `ao-core` lives at e.g. `/opt/homebrew/lib/node_modules/@composio/ao/node_modules/@composio/ao-core`, not inside `ao-web/node_modules/`. The check always fails.

## Environment

- macOS (Darwin 25.3.0, Apple Silicon)
- Node.js 20+
- pnpm 10.x
- `ao` installed globally via npm (`npm install -g @composio/ao`)

## Steps to Reproduce

1. Install `ao` globally: `npm install -g @composio/ao`
2. Create an `agent-orchestrator.yaml` config with one or more projects
3. Run `ao start <project-name>`
4. Observe the "Dependencies not installed" error

## Proposed Fix

Replace the hardcoded single-directory check with a **walk-up `node_modules` resolution**, which mirrors Node's own module resolution algorithm:

```ts
async function checkBuilt(webDir: string): Promise<void> {
  const corePkgDir = findPackageUp(webDir, "@composio", "ao-core");
  if (!corePkgDir) {
    throw new Error("Dependencies not installed. Run: pnpm install && pnpm build");
  }
  const coreEntry = resolve(corePkgDir, "dist", "index.js");
  if (!existsSync(coreEntry)) {
    throw new Error("Packages not built. Run: pnpm build");
  }
}

function findPackageUp(startDir: string, ...segments: string[]): string | null {
  let dir = resolve(startDir);
  const root = dirname(dir) === dir ? dir : undefined;
  while (true) {
    const candidate = resolve(dir, "node_modules", ...segments);
    if (existsSync(candidate)) return candidate;
    const parent = dirname(dir);
    if (parent === dir || parent === root) break;
    dir = parent;
  }
  return null;
}
```

This works correctly with:
- **pnpm workspaces** — finds `ao-core` symlinked in `<webDir>/node_modules/`
- **npm global installs** — finds `ao-core` hoisted to a parent `node_modules/`
- **yarn** — same hoisting behavior as npm

### Why not `createRequire().resolve()`?

`@composio/ao-core`'s `package.json` has an `exports` field that does not expose `./package.json` as a subpath, and only defines `import` conditions (not `require`). This makes `require.resolve()` unusable for locating the package directory.

## Files to Change

| File | Change |
|------|--------|
| `packages/cli/src/lib/preflight.ts` | Replace hardcoded path with `findPackageUp` walk-up resolution |
| `packages/cli/__tests__/lib/preflight.test.ts` | Update mocks and assertions to match new implementation |

## Bug

`ao start` fails with:

```
Error: Dependencies not installed. Run: pnpm install && pnpm build
```

even after a successful `pnpm install && pnpm build` of the user's project.

## Root Cause

The preflight check in `packages/cli/src/lib/preflight.ts` (`checkBuilt`) uses a hardcoded path to verify `@composio/ao-core` exists:

```ts
const nodeModules = resolve(webDir, "node_modules", "@composio", "ao-core");
if (!existsSync(nodeModules)) {
  throw new Error("Dependencies not installed. Run: pnpm install && pnpm build");
}
```

When `ao` is installed globally via `npm install -g @composio/ao`, npm **hoists** `@composio/ao-core` to the parent `node_modules/@composio/ao-core` rather than nesting it inside `ao-web/node_modules/@composio/ao-core`. The `existsSync` check fails because it only looks in the nested location.

**Hoisted (actual) location:**
```
node_modules/@composio/ao/node_modules/@composio/ao-core/dist/index.js  ✅ exists
```

**Expected by preflight:**
```
node_modules/@composio/ao/node_modules/@composio/ao-web/node_modules/@composio/ao-core  ❌ does not exist
```

## Suggested Fix

Replace the `existsSync` check with Node's module resolution, which respects hoisting:

```ts
import { createRequire } from "node:module";

async function checkBuilt(webDir: string) {
  const require = createRequire(resolve(webDir, "package.json"));
  try {
    require.resolve("@composio/ao-core");
  } catch {
    throw new Error("Dependencies not installed. Run: pnpm install && pnpm build");
  }

  // Also verify dist output exists
  const corePkg = resolve(require.resolve("@composio/ao-core/package.json"), "..");
  const coreEntry = resolve(corePkg, "dist", "index.js");
  if (!existsSync(coreEntry)) {
    throw new Error("Packages not built. Run: pnpm build");
  }
}
```

Alternatively, check both the nested and hoisted paths:

```ts
const nested = resolve(webDir, "node_modules", "@composio", "ao-core");
const hoisted = resolve(webDir, "..", "ao-core");
if (!existsSync(nested) && !existsSync(hoisted)) {
  throw new Error("Dependencies not installed. Run: pnpm install && pnpm build");
}
```

## Workaround

Symlink the hoisted package to where the check expects it:

```bash
WEB_DIR=$(node -e "const r=require('module').createRequire(import.meta?.url??__filename);console.log(require('path').dirname(r.resolve('@composio/ao-web/package.json')))" 2>/dev/null || echo "$(npm root -g)/@composio/ao/node_modules/@composio/ao-web")

mkdir -p "$WEB_DIR/node_modules/@composio"
ln -s "$(dirname "$WEB_DIR")/ao-core" "$WEB_DIR/node_modules/@composio/ao-core"
```

## Environment

- ao version: 0.1.0
- Node: $(node --version)
- npm: $(npm --version)
- OS: macOS (Darwin 25.3.0)
- Install method: `npm install -g @composio/ao`

# Design Doc: Worker Session Persistence on Respawn

**Author:** adil (adil.shaikh__)  
**Date:** 2026-03-28  
**Status:** Draft  
**Related Issues:** #183 (Codex getRestoreCommand — already implemented), #161 (resume workarounds), #138 (historical session visibility)

---

## 1. Problem Statement

When an AO worker session dies (crash, rate limit, OOM, manual kill) and is respawned for the same issue, the new worker starts from scratch with zero conversation history. The worker has to:

- Re-read the entire issue
- Re-analyze the codebase
- Re-discover what approach to take
- Potentially repeat the exact same mistake that caused the previous failure

This wastes tokens, time, and compute. For complex issues where a worker was 80% done, respawn means starting from 0%.

**The core problem:** The resume infrastructure exists in the agent plugins (`getRestoreCommand()` for Claude Code and Codex), but `spawn()` never uses it. Only the manual `ao session restore` path calls `getRestoreCommand()`. The automated respawn path always starts fresh.

---

## 2. Current Architecture

### 2.1 Session Lifecycle

```
ao start
  └─ spawnOrchestrator()          # Always uses getLaunchCommand() — fresh start
       └─ Orchestrator reads issue backlog
            └─ spawn(issueId)      # Always uses getLaunchCommand() — fresh start
                 └─ Worker starts from scratch

ao session restore <id>
  └─ restore()                     # Uses getRestoreCommand() — resumes conversation ✅
       └─ Worker picks up where it left off
```

The gap: `spawn()` and `spawnOrchestrator()` never call `getRestoreCommand()`, even when a previous session for the same issue exists on disk.

### 2.2 What Each Agent Supports Today

| Agent | `getRestoreCommand()` | Resume Command | Session Storage |
|---|---|---|---|
| Claude Code | ✅ Implemented | `claude --resume <uuid>` | `~/.claude/projects/{path}/*.jsonl` |
| Codex | ✅ Implemented | `codex resume <threadId>` | `~/.codex/sessions/*.jsonl` |
| OpenCode | ❌ Not implemented | N/A (uses session ID reuse during spawn) | Internal DB |
| Aider | ❌ Not implemented | N/A | `.aider.chat.history.md` in workspace |

### 2.3 Data That Persists on Disk After Kill

**Session Metadata** (`~/.agent-orchestrator/{hash}/sessions/`):
- Flat key-value files per session: worktree, branch, issue, PR, agent, status, timestamps, runtime handle, opencode session ID
- On `deleteMetadata(sessionsDir, id, archive=true)`: copied to `sessions/archive/{id}_{timestamp}` before deletion
- Readable via `readArchivedMetadataRaw(sessionsDir, id)`

**Git Worktrees:**
- Created at workspace path (e.g., `.git/worktrees/{branch-name}`)
- Contain all code changes, commits, staged files from previous worker
- Survive process death — they're just directories on disk
- Branch name derived from issue: `feat/{issueId}-{slug}` or `feat/{issueId}`

**Agent Session Files:**
- Claude Code: `~/.claude/projects/{encoded-workspace-path}/*.jsonl` — full conversation with tool calls, reasoning, outputs
- Codex: `~/.codex/sessions/*.jsonl` — thread data with model info
- Aider: `.aider.chat.history.md` in the workspace directory

**GitHub State:**
- PRs, issue comments, CI results — fully independent of AO runtime

### 2.4 Key Code Paths

**`spawn()` — `packages/core/src/session-manager.ts` line ~893:**
```typescript
async function spawn(spawnConfig: SessionSpawnConfig): Promise<Session> {
  // ... resolves plugins, creates worktree, builds prompt ...

  const launchCommand = plugins.agent.getLaunchCommand(agentLaunchConfig);  // ← ALWAYS fresh
  // Never checks for archived sessions
  // Never calls getRestoreCommand()
}
```

**`spawnOrchestrator()` — line ~1196:**
```typescript
async function spawnOrchestrator(orchestratorConfig: OrchestratorSpawnConfig): Promise<Session> {
  // ... checks if runtime is alive ...
  // If dead: archives metadata (if reuse strategy), deletes, reserves new ID

  const launchCommand = plugins.agent.getLaunchCommand(agentLaunchConfig);  // ← ALWAYS fresh
  // Has the archived metadata but doesn't use it for restore
}
```

**`restore()` — line ~2199 (the working path we want to replicate):**
```typescript
async function restore(sessionId: SessionId): Promise<Session> {
  // ... finds session metadata (active or archived) ...
  // ... validates restorability, rebuilds workspace ...

  if (plugins.agent.getRestoreCommand) {
    const restoreCmd = await plugins.agent.getRestoreCommand(session, project);  // ← USES RESUME ✅
    launchCommand = restoreCmd ?? plugins.agent.getLaunchCommand(agentLaunchConfig);
  }
}
```

### 2.5 Metadata Archive System

